<section class="section-dark">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="grid-head reveal">
<h2 class="h2">Questions about DJT / PBI transfers</h2>
<div class="rule"></div>
</div>
<div class="faq reveal">
<details><summary>Do I book under the DJT code or the old PBI code?<span class="pm">+</span></summary><div class="ans">Either works for the transfer itself — same airport, same terminal, same runways. Search whichever code your airline or booking site still shows; it makes no difference to pickup.</div></details>
<details><summary>Where at DJT / PBI do you meet arriving passengers?<span class="pm">+</span></summary><div class="ans">Curbside at arrivals by default, confirmed with you at booking. The terminal here is small enough that a meet-and-greet inside is rarely necessary, but it's available if you prefer it.</div></details>
<details><summary>How far is DJT / PBI from Aventura and Miami-Dade?<span class="pm">+</span></summary><div class="ans">It's the longest of the three airports we cover — figure roughly 55–70 minutes down to Aventura outside peak traffic. One chauffeur covers the whole corridor, so a trip that starts here and continues south stays with the same driver.</div></details>
<details><summary>Do you serve Palm Beach and Boca Raton directly from this airport?<span class="pm">+</span></summary><div class="ans">Yes. Palm Beach itself and Boca Raton are both short, routine runs from DJT — typically well under 30 minutes — well before continuing further south becomes relevant.</div></details>
<details><summary>Is a private chauffeur worth it for a smaller airport like this?<span class="pm">+</span></summary><div class="ans">It's arguably more useful here than at MIA or FLL — rideshare and taxi availability at DJT can be thinner outside peak hours, and a pre-arranged pickup removes that uncertainty entirely.</div></details>
</div>
</div>
